  steps: [
    { math: '1 · The atmosphere is thin and layered', note: 'Half its mass lies below ~5.5 km (Mount Everest\'s summit pokes above half the air). Layers by temperature: troposphere (weather), stratosphere (ozone), mesosphere, thermosphere.' },
    { math: '2 · Weather is heat redistribution', note: 'Tropics get surplus energy, poles deficit → the atmosphere (and ocean) move heat poleward. Circulation cells (Hadley, Ferrel, Polar) + Earth\'s rotation (Coriolis) = the trade winds, westerlies, and the jet streams riding the boundaries.' },
    { math: '3 · Fronts and lows: the storm factory', note: 'Mid-latitude weather is the battle of air masses: cyclones spin up along fronts (warm air overriding cold = clouds and rain). In the tropics, warm ocean + rotation = hurricanes (warm-core engines — the monsoon lesson\'s cyclones).' },
    { math: '4 · Forecasting is physics plus data', note: 'Numerical weather prediction (Richardson\'s dream, 1922; operational by the 1950s): solve the fluid equations forward from current observations. Forecast skill doubles ~every decade — 5-day forecasts today beat 3-day forecasts from the 1980s.' },
  ],
};
