<TopicPage slug="subjects/earth-science/intermediate/atmosphere-and-weather-systems">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Weather is the atmosphere <strong>moving heat from where there is surplus to where there is
    deficit</strong>, with water as the carrier and rotation as the steering. Understand the
    three-moves — circulation cells, fronts, convection — and daily weather stops being
    mysterious.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the layers, the engine, the storm factory, the forecast" widget="equationStep" config={wxCfg} />

  <h2>Concepts with daily leverage</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Water\'s phase changes are the battery:</strong> evaporation stores latent heat, condensation releases it — a single thunderstorm releases energy comparable to a nuclear weapon (uncontrolled, unfocused — which is why we fear neither).</li>
    <li><strong>Reading a weather map:</strong> isobars tight = windy; fronts bring the change; low pressure = rising air = cloud and rain; high = sinking = clear. Five rules cover most synoptic maps.</li>
    <li><strong>Extreme weather attribution is new science:</strong> since ~2004, studies quantify climate change\'s fingerprint on specific events (World Weather Attribution) — "this heatwave was ~X× more likely" — moving the climate debate from models to measured weather.</li>
    <li><strong>Ensemble forecasting changed decisions:</strong> running the model 50 times with perturbed inputs gives probabilities ("70% chance of rain") — the statistics ladder\'s uncertainty thinking, applied to tomorrow.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Richardson, Weather Prediction by Numerical Process (1922); NOAA/NWS; World Weather Attribution (2021–); the monsoon lesson for tropical circulation.</p>
</TopicPage>
